
  [1mClassification: architectural.[0m I know that sounds heavy for moving one string, but the request names a new module this
  repo doesn't have, and the repo has no module wiring for browser code to hang it on — so the real question isn't
  "where does the constant live," it's "how does a script-tag page load config and how does an environment get
  selected." That's new structure, not a change to an existing flow. I'll do the full process: questions, approaches, a
  spec, then a plan. Say the word if you'd rather I treat it as a one-line move and skip the ceremony.

  First question — this is the fork everything else hangs off.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how the environment gets chosen at runtime. Not where the setting lives, but who decides which value is
  live.

  - [1mHand-edited single value[0m — one [38;5;153msettings.js[39m with the endpoint; you edit it when you deploy elsewhere. Cheapest, zero
    new tooling. Costs: the edit is a tracked file change, so dev and prod configs fight in git. Easy to change later.
  - [1mHostname detection[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m maps [38;5;153mlocation.hostname[39m to an endpoint; no edit needed to switch. Costs: all
    environments' URLs ship to the browser, and the map has to be kept current. Also easy to change later.
  - [1mBuild-time injection[0m — a build step substitutes the value per environment. Most "correct," and the only one that
    keeps prod URLs out of dev bundles. Costs: introduces a build step and a package manager to a repo that currently
    has neither — that one is not cheap to undo.

  [1mRecommendation: hostname detection.[0m It matches the stated goal ("easier to change environments") without adding a
  toolchain to a repo that has no [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m, and it degrades to the hand-edited case if you only list one host.
